        "city": "Dallas",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the Dallas-Fort Worth market",
        "market_text": "<p>Dallas–Fort Worth adds more people each year than almost any metro in the country, and a large share of them are relocating on a corporate timeline from California, Illinois or the Northeast. They build a shortlist online weeks before they fly in, and an empty room gives them nothing to judge but square footage.</p><p>The other half of the market is new construction. Plano, Frisco, McKinney and Prosper turn over thousands of spec homes and model-plan builds where six listings in a development share three floor plans. Photographed empty, they're indistinguishable — home staging is what makes one of them the listing a relocating buyer remembers.</p><p>DFW price points also make the maths obvious. Physical staging is priced for furniture plus a monthly rental period regardless of how long the listing sits. Virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, once, with no rental clock — which matters on a builder's inventory of eight vacant homes rather than one.</p><p>That's why Dallas virtual staging is routine for DFW agents, builders and investors. 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search (NAR), so the listing photos are where most buyers first compare homes — and listings that stand out online tend to draw more clicks, views and showing requests. Virtually staged photos show what each room is for and how much furniture it really holds, so the house makes the shortlist before anyone books a showing. For a relocating buyer deciding which homes to tour on a single trip, that is often the whole decision.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Dallas home staging, done virtually for each buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage modern-transitional for Plano, Frisco and McKinney new builds, warmer traditional for Highland Park and Lakewood, and clean contemporary for Uptown and Design District condos.</p><p>Builder and investor inventory is quoted as a batch and delivered together, so a whole community's worth of listings lands at once rather than photo by photo. Files come back NTREIS-ready with disclosure guidance included.</p><p>Our design team works online, so there's no walkthrough to schedule anywhere in Texas: order, upload your photos and a designer handles the styling. Volume discounts apply automatically — 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+ — which is where most Plano and Frisco builder sets land. Modern and transitional styles suit most DFW new construction, while Lakewood Tudors and Craftsman bungalows get warmer traditional pieces in wood tones.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Plano, Frisco, McKinney, Allen, Prosper, Uptown, Highland Park, Lakewood, Arlington, Fort Worth"
      }
    },
